# The two-hourly sweep decision, run by BOTH implementations of the rule:
#
#   lifecycle::sweep_action         (Rust, fleet-view/)  via fleet-view/src/lifecycle.rs
#   cerebro--sweep-action           (emacs/cerebro.el)   via emacs/cerebro-test.el
#
# WHY IT IS ONE FILE. `tests/lib/triage.cases`' reason exactly: until the declaration moves both
# views supervise the same checkout, and a row the two answer differently is a Cerebro told to
# sweep twice in one window, or never told at all - neither of which announces itself, because a
# typed line is fire-and-forget and Cerebro writes nothing back to say it heard.
#
# IT IS A SEPARATE TABLE FROM `triage.cases` ON PURPOSE. Triage's trigger is a CONDITION that
# stays true, so a busy Cerebro needs no queue. A two-hour mark is an EDGE that passes, and if it
# passes while Cerebro is working there is nothing left to observe next tick - which is the whole
# reason the `pending' column exists here and in no sibling table.
#
# WHAT BOTH READERS MUST BIND. The rows assume `cerebro-sweep-interval' / `SWEEP_INTERVAL_SECONDS'
# is 7200, so a reader binds or passes exactly that number rather than whatever the running fleet
# is customised to.
#
# THE COLUMNS, tab- or space-separated, in order:
#
#   role            The roster role. Only `orchestrator' is ever told.
#   kind            `interactive' or `implementer' - the roster kind.
#   state           The derived row state: `idle' `working' `asking' `waiting' `up' `unknown'
#                   `dead' `standby'. Only an exact `idle' is ever told.
#   hosted          `yes' when THIS view holds a live session for the agent it could type into.
#                   Rust's `SessionHost::supervisable'; Emacs's `cerebro--session' answering a
#                   live buffer. NOT the elisp triage teller's weaker `external' test.
#   since_mark_s    Seconds since this name's mark - the last sweep line typed into it, or the
#                   first tick this view saw a session for it. `-' when this view holds no mark.
#   pending         `yes' when a mark has already passed while this name was not idle.
#   expect          `tell' `queue' `forget' `mark' or `none'.
#
#   `tell'   type the line, move the mark to now, clear the pending flag.
#   `queue'  set the pending flag; type nothing, and leave the mark where it is.
#   `forget' drop this name's mark and pending flag; type nothing.
#   `mark'   start the clock silently at now; type nothing.
#   `none'   do nothing at all - neither type, nor record, nor forget.

# role          kind         state    hosted since_mark_s pending expect

# Not an orchestrator, not an interactive kind: nothing recorded, nothing forgotten.
planner         interactive  idle     yes    0            no      none
implementer     interactive  idle     yes    7200         no      none
orchestrator    implementer  idle     yes    7200         no      none

# No session this view hosts: no clock, and any mark or queued line is dropped. This is what
# keeps a restarted Cerebro from being told to sweep seconds after its own startup sweep.
orchestrator    interactive  idle     no     7200         no      forget
orchestrator    interactive  idle     no     -            no      forget
orchestrator    interactive  dead     no     30000        yes     forget

# A hosted session with no mark: the clock starts, silently, whatever the state.
orchestrator    interactive  idle     yes    -            no      mark
orchestrator    interactive  working  yes    -            no      mark

# Before the mark, nothing. `>=', so 7200 exactly tells.
orchestrator    interactive  idle     yes    0            no      none
orchestrator    interactive  idle     yes    7199         no      none
orchestrator    interactive  idle     yes    7200         no      tell
orchestrator    interactive  idle     yes    10800        no      tell

# A mark that passes while Cerebro is not idle is queued and never typed.
orchestrator    interactive  working  yes    7200         no      queue
orchestrator    interactive  asking   yes    7200         no      queue
orchestrator    interactive  waiting  yes    7200         no      queue
orchestrator    interactive  up       yes    7200         no      queue
orchestrator    interactive  unknown  yes    7200         no      queue
orchestrator    interactive  standby  yes    7200         no      queue
orchestrator    interactive  dead     yes    7200         no      queue

# A queued line waits for idle however long that takes, and is typed then - even though the mark
# itself is fresh, which is what "queued" means.
orchestrator    interactive  working  yes    30000        yes     queue
orchestrator    interactive  idle     yes    30000        yes     tell
orchestrator    interactive  idle     yes    10           yes     tell

# A flag and not a count: a second mark passing while it is set changes nothing.
orchestrator    interactive  working  yes    20000        yes     queue
